title: 'What Jeff calls a lack, that skg "is only currently useable with the help of AI", is what Grudin found every successful group tool was sold with: someone beside the new user.'
pid: fd240b6d-844a-4d89-8931-3492889d6136
body: |2-
  'docs/README.org' says it, then lists questions to "just ask" an AI.
  His eighth cause: a group tool sold off the shelf and left to its
  buyers fails; the successes, Lotus Notes and IBM's TeamFocus, were
  sold with consultants who chose the first users, trained them, and
  caught early troubles before they became rejection. His fifth wishes
  for "systems that take the initiative to educate users over time",
  work that "mostly in AI, has proceeded slowly". An assistant that
  answers a newcomer's questions about skg is that consultant, at a
  price no vendor of 1994 could offer. So it is the way a stranger
  arrives, not a stopgap until the docs suffice.
